  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual watershed diagram showing how radioactive cesium, labeled as ^137Cs, moves through a landscape from land surfaces into rivers, groundwater, and ultimately the ocean. The central message is that different land uses and vegetation types control how cesium is intercepted, transferred, and redistributed.\n\nIn forested areas, cesium is first caught by the tree canopy and then reaches the ground through stemflow and throughfall, with cedar and broadleaf forests illustrated as examples. From there, it appears to migrate downward into soil and also be carried laterally into streams within a forested catchment. Agricultural zones such as paddy fields, farmland, and grassland contribute cesium to rivers mainly through soil erosion and runoff. An urban area is also shown as a source of contaminated discharge.\n\nOnce cesium enters the river network, it is transported downstream with water and suspended sediment, eventually discharging into the ocean. The figure also highlights subsurface movement, including downward migration in soils and groundwater monitoring. Small insets suggest field methods for sampling soil and measuring suspended sediment and water at upstream, midstream, and downstream locations. Overall, the image summarizes linked terrestrial, riverine, and coastal transport pathways.",
